(ns kotoba.compiler.accelerator-simdgroup-reduce
  "The Metal-native SIMD-group reduce variant (:simdgroup-reduce, ADR-0373,
  JUN-58) -- the reduce shape (:sum/:max/:min, W >= 32) on the MSL ROUTE
  ONLY. Its intra-SIMD-group fold is Metal's own SIMD-group reduction
  intrinsics (simd_sum / simd_max / simd_min), followed by the SAME
  cross-SIMD-group tree over the W/S partials that the shared
  :subgroup-reduce fold uses.

  STATE, exactly (ADR-0373's rule-3 order: step 1 landed 2026-10-23 and,
  in the later same-day slice, step 2): the
  EMISSION is implemented here with tests and ADMITTED into
  kotoba.compiler.accelerator's plumbing -- `lower` answers this text for
  the (:msl-v1, :simdgroup-reduce) pair in both readings, so compile-kernel
  seals it and verify-artifact! re-lowers it like any other emission --
  and the SELECTION predicate landed with it: kotoba.compiler.accelerator-
  variants' select-variant answers :simdgroup-reduce under ADR-0373's MSL
  ladder (rule 3 step 2) -- first rung for an Apple MSL descriptor
  reporting the fold's rule-6 fact (:simd-reduce) and a reported
  SIMD-group width dividing W, outside the measured-class carve-out --
  and every such answer is UNMEASURED (:measured? false) except this
  variant's OWN flip cells. Rule 3 step 3 (measurement) STARTED
  2026-10-23: the native-Metal channel ran :baseline vs THIS variant at
  W = 256, n = 1000003 on Apple M1 Max and measured it FASTER (paired
  median ratios 0.790/0.800/0.799 for :sum/:max/:min, claim-protocol
  rule 2 REGIME-INVARIANT FASTER, 18/18 per-run ratios below 1.0), the
  W-sweep series' second cell the same at W = 512 (paired median
  ratios 0.815/0.823/0.824, rule 2 REGIME-INVARIANT FASTER, 18/18), the
  series' third cell the same at W = 1024 (paired median ratios
  0.840/0.847/0.850, rule 2 REGIME-INVARIANT FASTER, 18/18, 2026-10-24)
  and the series' fourth cell the same at W = 128 (paired median ratios
  0.775/0.789/0.789, rule 2 REGIME-INVARIANT FASTER, 18/18, 2026-10-24)
  and the series' fifth cell the same at W = 64 (paired median ratios
  0.719/0.728/0.729, rule 2 REGIME-INVARIANT FASTER, 18/18, 2026-10-24)
  and the series' sixth and last cell the same at W = 32 (paired median
  ratios 0.657/0.658/0.650, rule 2 REGIME-INVARIANT FASTER, 18/18,
  2026-10-24)
  --
  the
  flip contract answers those cells :simdgroup-reduce with :measured?
  true
  (SIX cells measured -- the class's whole W band [32, 1024]; shape 2
  measured at W = 256, the shape-2 flip of that cell
  (2026-10-24), and at W = 512 measured 2026-10-24 but REFUSED as a
  selection basis (claim-protocol rule 2 REGIME-SCOPED -- the first
  measured-but-scoped cell; the W = 512 record's :shapes stays
  [1000003]), and every other device
  UNMEASURED).

  What the emission is (ADR-0373's decision, to its letter): the
  intra-SIMD-group stage of :subgroup-reduce's MSL arm -- the
  simd_shuffle_down ladder -- is replaced by ONE SIMD-group reduction
  intrinsic per operator (simd_sum / simd_max / simd_min); everything above
  it is the shared fold, unchanged: the partials array sized from the
  emitted W, the one write barrier, the strided cross-SIMD-group tree.
  P2 (partial compaction) binds unchanged: the partial index is the
  SIMD-group index (simdgroup_index_in_threadgroup written only by the
  thread_index_in_simdgroup == 0 lane), never lid arithmetic; the
  S-strided tree over the dense partial array stands. P1 binds by option
  (b): simd_sum's association order inside the SIMD-group is out of the
  emitter's hands and is not pinned by the cited vendor documentation, so
  the :sum claim is scoped to low bits for inputs without catastrophic
  cancellation and the cancellation-sensitive regime is an UNMEASURED
  divergence from :baseline; :max/:min keep the bit-for-bit rule within the
  ordinary-value domain only (non-NaN payloads, no negative zeros).

  Safety: the row admits W a power of two in [32, 1024] (the shared
  min-workgroup-size 32 floor). A Metal SIMD-group size is a power of two,
  so every SIMD-group of the threadgroup is either full or the single first
  partial one (and a workgroup the reported SIMD-group width does not
  divide answers :baseline at SELECTION time -- rule 1, that gate is
  kotoba.compiler.accelerator-variants' simd-reduce gate, landed with
  rule 3 step 2). The padded lanes (i >= n) fold the operator's identity
  before the intrinsic, so the SIMD-group fold sees exactly the admitted
  elements in every lane.

  ABI, unchanged from the MSL family: x 0, parts 1, n 2
  (constant uint& n [[buffer(2)]]), the partials array is an in-kernel
  threadgroup float partials[W], and the entry point is the kernel's
  :kernel/name -- Metal refuses a kernel named main (compiler-verified in
  kotoba.compiler.accelerator-subgroup-reduce's MSL arm). This emission
  needs no fold-width query (there is no shuffle ladder to guard), so
  threads_per_threadgroup does not appear; the subgroup geometry
  (thread_index_in_simdgroup / simdgroup_index_in_threadgroup /
  threads_per_simdgroup / simdgroups_per_threadgroup) is the shared
  fold's, unchanged.

  UNMEASURED except its flip cells: no speed claim exists for this variant
  anywhere but its own flip cells (W = 32, W = 64, W = 128, W = 256,
  W = 512
  and
  W = 1024,
  n = 1000003,
  Apple M1 Max --
  paired median ratios 0.657/0.658/0.650 at W = 32,
  0.719/0.728/0.729 at W = 64,
  0.775/0.789/0.789 at W = 128,
  0.790/0.800/0.799 at W = 256,
  0.815/0.823/0.824 at W = 512 and 0.840/0.847/0.850 at W = 1024,
  rule-2 REGIME-INVARIANT FASTER at all six
  cells,
  docs/evidence/gpu-simdgroup-reduce-m1max-wsweep-20261023/w32,
  docs/evidence/gpu-simdgroup-reduce-m1max-wsweep-20261023/w64,
  docs/evidence/gpu-simdgroup-reduce-m1max-wsweep-20261023/w128,
  docs/evidence/gpu-simdgroup-reduce-m1max-20261023,
  docs/evidence/gpu-simdgroup-reduce-m1max-wsweep-20261023/w512 and
  docs/evidence/gpu-simdgroup-reduce-m1max-wsweep-20261023/w1024). No
  Metal execution
  path exists in this repository (the brokers died with the C loader;
  ADR 0371's provider is the replacement track); the flip cells' numbers
  come from the native-Metal measurement harness (ADR-0360 question (1)'s
  conforming channel, the emitters' own bytes), not the product runtime.
  The emitted source COMPILES on this host under the
  Apple Metal compiler (a toolchain fact of what compiles, recorded in
  docs/evidence/gpu/simdgroup-reduce-msl-emission-20261023/, not a
  measurement). Every other answer this row carries stays
  :measured? false until a hardware measurement under ADR-0360's
  measurement contract replaces the basis text."
  {:kotoba/export [applicable? basis emit-msl min-workgroup-size]}
  (:require [kotoba.compiler.accelerator-kir :as kir]))

(def min-workgroup-size
  "The smallest workgroup this variant admits: the shared 32 floor of the
  reduce-variant family (ADR-0356's predicate). A smaller workgroup gets
  :baseline -- the fold would be over a partial SIMD-group only, where the
  tree is already shorter than the machinery."
  32)

(def basis
  "Why this variant exists. DESIGN ASSUMPTION, not measurement evidence,
  except at the measured cells. :measured? false is the honesty lock;
  it flips only when a hardware measurement under ADR-0360's measurement
  contract replaces :text for a named device class -- 2026-10-23/24
  flipped
  W = 32, W = 64, W = 128, W = 256, W = 512 and W = 1024 on Apple M1
  Max for this
  variant
  (measured FASTER
  than
  :baseline at all six cells, apple-simdgroup-flip-note in
  kotoba.compiler.accelerator-variants). The one device with any
  subgroup-reduce evidence (Apple M1 Max) refuted the SHUFFLE fold's
  rationale, and that refutation neither condemns nor blesses this
  variant (ADR-0373) -- this variant's own flip cells measured it
  directly."
  {:measured? false
   :text
   "Design assumption (UNMEASURED outside its flip cells): the baseline
    tree
    reduce pays log2(W) barrier stages; folding within a SIMD-group needs
    no barriers and no shared memory in the intra stage, leaving one
    write barrier plus a log2(W/S)-stage cross-SIMD-group tree. The intra
    stage here is ONE reduction intrinsic per operator (simd_sum /
    simd_max / simd_min) instead of the shuffle ladder -- a structural
    difference, priced at its flip cells only (W = 32, W = 64, W = 128,
    W = 256,
    W = 512 and W = 1024,
    n = 1000003, Apple M1
    Max, 2026-10-23/24: paired median ratios 0.657/0.658/0.650 at
    W = 32,
    0.719/0.728/0.729 at
    W = 64,
    0.775/0.789/0.789 at
    W = 128, 0.790/0.800/0.799 at W = 256,
    0.815/0.823/0.824 at W = 512 and 0.840/0.847/0.850 at W = 1024 for
    :sum/:max/:min, claim-protocol rule 2 REGIME-INVARIANT FASTER at
    all six cells,
    docs/evidence/gpu-simdgroup-reduce-m1max-wsweep-20261023/w32,
    docs/evidence/gpu-simdgroup-reduce-m1max-wsweep-20261023/w64,
    docs/evidence/gpu-simdgroup-reduce-m1max-wsweep-20261023/w128,
    docs/evidence/gpu-simdgroup-reduce-m1max-20261023,
    docs/evidence/gpu-simdgroup-reduce-m1max-wsweep-20261023/w512 and
    docs/evidence/gpu-simdgroup-reduce-m1max-wsweep-20261023/w1024).
    Everywhere else
    nothing has measured simd_sum against the shuffle loop, and the
    shuffle fold's own rationale measured CONTRADICTED on the one device
    tested (M1 Max). No speed claim outside the flip cells' own recorded
    scope."})

(defn applicable?
  "TOTAL predicate for the row ADR-0373 specifies: a REDUCE kernel over
  :sum/:max/:min whose workgroup is a power of two in [32, 1024] -- the
  same row shape as :subgroup-reduce's, one variant per fold. Everything
  else -- ewise, a sub-warp workgroup, a malformed or partial kernel map --
  answers false, never a throw. Selection time is where a refusal costs
  the most."
  [kir]
  (and (map? kir)
       (= :reduce (:kernel/op kir))
       (contains? #{:sum :max :min} (:kernel/operator kir))
       (let [wg (:kernel/workgroup-size kir)]
         (and (int? wg)
              (pos? wg)
              (zero? (bit-and wg (dec wg)))
              (<= wg kir/max-workgroup-size)
              (>= wg min-workgroup-size)))))

(def ^:private identity-value-msl
  ;; The same INFINITE neutrals as every other arm (byte-identical to
  ;; accelerator-subgroup-reduce's identity-value-msl -- one meaning per
  ;; operator in every arm): MSL has no infinity literal, as_type of the
  ;; IEEE-754 bits is exact.
  {:sum "0.0f"
   :max "as_type<float>(0xFF800000u)"
   :min "as_type<float>(0x7F800000u)"})

(def ^:private simd-reduce-intrinsic
  ;; The intra-SIMD-group fold: ONE Metal SIMD-group reduction intrinsic
  ;; per operator (ADR-0373's decision). The tree above keeps the family's
  ;; combine expression; the intrinsic IS the intra-stage combine.
  {:sum "simd_sum" :max "simd_max" :min "simd_min"})

(def ^:private combine-expression-msl
  {:sum "a+b" :max "max(a,b)" :min "min(a,b)"})

(defn emit-msl
  "The :simdgroup-reduce MSL compute shader for one validated KIR. The
  shared fold of kotoba.compiler.accelerator-subgroup-reduce's MSL arm with
  the intra-SIMD-group stage replaced by one reduction intrinsic per
  operator (ADR-0373). Total on the row, fail-closed off it: a kernel that
  `applicable?` refuses is refused here too (ex-info \"kernel variant not
  applicable\") -- silently answering another fold would make the artifact
  claim a variant it does not carry. A malformed KIR is refused by the same
  validate! the ADR-0002 emitters use. Deterministic: the same KIR lowers
  to the same bytes, forever. The entry point is the kernel's :kernel/name
  (Metal refuses a kernel named main -- the MSL family's own entry
  contract)."
  [kir]
  (kir/validate! kir)
  (when-not (applicable? kir)
    (throw (ex-info "kernel variant not applicable"
                    {:variant :simdgroup-reduce
                     :op (:kernel/op kir)
                     :operator (:kernel/operator kir)
                     :workgroup-size (:kernel/workgroup-size kir)})))
  (let [wg (:kernel/workgroup-size kir)
        name (:kernel/name kir)
        operator (:kernel/operator kir)
        identity (identity-value-msl operator)
        intrinsic (simd-reduce-intrinsic operator)
        combine (combine-expression-msl operator)]
    (str "#include <metal_stdlib>\n"
         "using namespace metal;\n"
         "float combine(float a, float b) { return " combine "; }\n"
         "kernel void " name "(device const float* x [[buffer(0)]], device float* parts [[buffer(1)]], constant uint& n [[buffer(2)]], uint i [[thread_position_in_grid]], uint sgid [[thread_index_in_simdgroup]], uint sg [[simdgroup_index_in_threadgroup]], uint sgsize [[threads_per_simdgroup]], uint nsg [[simdgroups_per_threadgroup]], uint g [[threadgroup_position_in_grid]]) {\n"
         "  threadgroup float partials[" wg "];\n"
         "  float v = i < n ? x[i] : " identity ";\n"
         ;; The intra-SIMD-group stage, ADR-0373: ONE reduction intrinsic
         ;; over the SIMD-group's active lanes. Every launched thread
         ;; reaches it (no early exit; the padded lanes fold the identity),
         ;; so the intrinsic folds exactly the admitted elements of this
         ;; SIMD-group -- and because a Metal SIMD-group size is a power of
         ;; two, every SIMD-group is full or the single first partial one.
         ;; simd_sum's association order is the vendor's own (P1 option b:
         ;; low-bits scope for :sum), and there is no shuffle ladder and no
         ;; fold width to guard.
         "  v = " intrinsic "(v);\n"
         "  if (sgid == 0u) { partials[sg] = v; }\n"
         "  threadgroup_barrier(mem_flags::mem_threadgroup);\n"
         ;; The shared cross-SIMD-group fold, byte-for-byte the
         ;; subgroup-reduce MSL arm's: the strided tree over the nsg
         ;; partials (P2: the partial index is the SUBGROUP index, never lid
         ;; arithmetic); nsg is 1 or a power of two (W and the SIMD-group
         ;; size are), so the halving tree is exact. The barrier per stage
         ;; is uniform -- every thread of the threadgroup reaches it.
         "  for (uint d = nsg / 2u; d > 0u; d >>= 1u) {\n"
         "    if (sg == 0u) {\n"
         "      for (uint lid = sgid; lid < d; lid += sgsize) {\n"
         "        partials[lid] = combine(partials[lid], partials[lid + d]);\n"
         "      }\n"
         "    }\n"
         "    threadgroup_barrier(mem_flags::mem_threadgroup);\n"
         "  }\n"
         "  if (sg == 0u && sgid == 0u) { parts[g] = partials[0]; }\n"
         "}\n")))
